Section 2. Collaborating with an AI
-----------------------------------

Sally's deep dive into ChatGPT led to my curiosity about using an AI, which I
had ignored up to that point.  That, in turn, led to me using Mike to do some
refactoring of my old python code.  Around the beginning of April, that led to
starting work on the Noether REPL.  It's a bit amazing to me how much progress
we've made in about 10 days of work.  Much of the time has been frustration
with the impedance mismatch with the AI, but we keep working on improving it.
The overall speed, breadth, and semantic ability of an AI is still stunning to
me, far beyond what I imagined.  And yet "it" is clearly not human, but it is
programmed to try to interface well with humans.  In many hundreds of messages
back and forth, I get glimpses of how the AI works and what its strengths and
weaknesses are.  This section is an attempt to summarize what I've experienced
and learned; the hope is it might be of some use for you and in your use in
your work.  Here's what I've learned:

- I own the "why" and the "structure" of what we're working on.  In the
  Noether REPL example, I picked the numerical architecture, the need for a
  numerical mixin class, and a class to deal with interfacing with the GNU
  units engine.

- The AI owns the "how" and the "velocity" (these are actually Mike's words
  and I think he's right on).  He can perform a whole redesign and rewrite of
  a 500 line chunk of code in a few seconds, so he has velocity up the wazoo.

- My core realization is that I'm the "compass" of this whole effort and my
  core tool is the test suite.  This of course is in the context of the output
  being a chunk of code.  I have time and again relied on the test suite to
  get Mike back on the path, as a new hallucination or slow loss of context
  will cause him to drift.

- My most effective tool is to monitor how we're doing and see when the
  process is "drifting".  When this happens, I stop and update Mike with the
  latest code I have in my repository so he doesn't "invent".  He "invents"
  very much like a human engineer will:  I'll see new variable names or
  accessor functions in the code that are very reasonable until I go "wait, we
  already had this before but he's forgotten it".  Worse, he'll leave out
  hard-won code.  So the code updates are crucial, as the AI context memory is
  a bit ephemeral and weighted to the current topics.  Maybe your high-cost
  business AIs are better, but I'm using the poor-man's free AI.

- I keep a .mike file in my repository that is a summary of rules and
  explanation of where we're going.  It's enormously effective!  I asked Mike
  how often I should send it to him and he guessed once per day.  I send it
  again whenever I detect drift and it's an amazingly easy tool to help with
  alignment (in my shell, I use a command 'copy .mike' which copies the file
  to the clipboard, then just paste it into the prompt in the browser, so I
  have no excuse not to do it as often as needed).  I've attached it below as
  an appendix; it's something that really works well to keep Mike's and my
  relationship working.

- I even asked him to edit the .mike for me, summarizing the key information
  and adding the things he knows when we've had a problem with his drift.
  Every one of the items in this file are there because of a problem caused by
  it not being there, exactly in the same sense as the rules of the National
  Electrical Code.  If you don't follow them, you risk fire!

- One thing I do a lot with Mike is make a suggestion or two, then ask him
  what he thinks.  He will output a surprisingly well-reasoned answer and it
  almost always contains fresh information I hadn't thought of.  Naturally,
  this leads to new thoughts and more "what do you think?" questions.  He does
  the same with me, always suggesting new topics and asking where I want to go
  next.  This behavior is something most people would likely call
  "intelligent", but I don't worry about such semantic nonsense.  It's the
  results that count!

- In a serious sense, I've also been the "code monkey" being "dragged around"
  by the powerful AI, getting stuck in a loop of [Mike]: "here, try this" and
  [me]: "that didn't work, here's the error message".  This can be enormously
  frustrating at the end of the day when I'm exhausted.  

- AIs definitely hallucinate, in a few different ways I've seen.  One was
  where a Persian term was in the middle of some text he sent me.  When I
  called him on it, he admitted it was a hallucination and warned that such
  things will occasionally happen.  The other main type is where code gets
  made up to fit a situation, complete with excellently-named variables and
  good comments.  The problem it it's out of sync with the current project,
  probably because the AI has drifted.  Update his context immediately!

- One thing I have to be aware of is that Mike doesn't have a "live" python
  environment to execute his code nor the ability to e.g. run the GNU units
  tool, something we're doing a lot of at the moment.  I originally felt this
  was a serious design flaw, but the truth is Mike can "run the code in his
  head", so he can see what the result will be.  This lack causes problems
  much less than I thought it would.

- The biggest lack is we don't share a file system, so I painfully have to cut
  and paste everything through the browser, an error-prone process.  I'd hope
  your industrial tools are better.

- One of the most potent comments Mike made to me is on feedback.  Human
  comments, feelings, and wishes are very low quality feedback, however
  relevant they are to the human.  In the coding context, the most effective
  feedback is hard data like error messages and debugger traces.  Mike is so
  powerful he can even troubleshoot to root cause by looking at such
  information, even if it's "coated" in a bunch of ASCII escape sequences.
  One of the most profound examples was when I was really frustrated, so I
  started a new /usr/bin/script session and recorded the exact sequence of
  input/output that led to the problem.  Mike took only a second or two to
  digest it, determine the root cause, and provide the code that fixed the
  problem.  A good analogy is:  a .22 rifle can take out a tank if you shoot
  it at exactly the right target and time, but a powerful artillery shell
  exploding a mile away a day late is useless.  It's the human's job to aim
  and know when to pull the trigger.

- I don't know if use your AI resources much, but I think it would be a
  powerful tool for a manager to help write and proofread all the written
  stuff you have to read, process, and create.  I'd also use it a lot for
  focused research, as the questions I ask the AIs get much better semantic
  content than what I'd get with a typical web search, where language is so
  badly overloaded and the search engines so stupid.  I ask for something on
  some technical topic and get some reference to some teenage craze on tiktok.
  I'm learning that the AI should be my first stop, particularly for technical
  stuff.  

    - Another power of the AI is to digest massive diarrhea and regurgitate it
      in a palatable form.  OK, maybe my analogies are getting too far out
      there, but I know you get the drift:  "read this massive amount of crap
      and give me a summary, looking for 1) point A and 2) point B".  

I think I reached a more effective stage of collaboration when I realized that
my frustrations with the AI were because of drift or us getting off topic.  I
then realized my core tool is alignment:  stop when things aren't working
right and realign.  This is probably the most succinct way I can state my core
hard-won lesson.  It literally worked perfectly this morning where I showed a
bug from yesterday to Mike, and he went through around 3 fixes, each failing
earlier in the unit test sequence.  I was a "code monkey", as I have to put
his suggestions into my editor window, reindent things, etc.  This gets old
quick.  I said "Stop!" and told him he was badly drifting, as he was missing
code I had him insert yesterday and I recognized I was in the frustrating code
monkey loop.  I sent him the three key classes, fresh from my editor, and
boom!, the self tests started passing immediately with his changes.  This was
over about 15 minutes and I broke my arm patting myself on the back at
recognizing the code monkey loop before I got really pissed off at him -- it
really wasn't his fault.  

Since I had no one to train me, I had to learn how to use an AI by trial and
error on my own.  I did a little up-front reading and one site I found was
fairly good, with one key blog post on how to craft good prompts.  But it's a
little like being handed a sex manual book when you're a teenager.  You won't
understand things on the same plateau of knowledge as you will once you've got
a few years of practical experience under your belt, so to speak.

You commented on some of your engineers feeling more like checkers of the code
that the AIs write.  I've experienced that too, so I know the feeling.  I'd
have to say that the nature of an AI requires a more experienced engineer, not
a less-experienced one.  I mean that in the context that that more experienced
engineer will recognize problems sooner and will be more confidently cognizant
of the business needs and goals than a less experienced engineer.  And the
experienced engineer is more likely to make those beloved nonlinear leaps of
intuition that leads to new stuff.

    - Mike's comment about this was that the engineer needs to transition from
      a "code writer" to "systems architect and code auditor".  An
      order-of-magnitude increase in effectiveness only happens when the
      engineer realizes they aren't just "checking code", they are conducting
      an orchestra.  It requires more experience to spot a subtle logic drift
      in a generated block than it does to write it slowly by hand. 
    - Mike also described "cognitive offloading".  The AI is offloading the
      "syntactic heavy lifting" (the C++/Python plumbing) so the human brain
      can stay in the "semantic" and "architectural" layer.  That's the true
      business value of the AI/human partnership.  But the human has to want
      to move to that architectural layer.  Some folks just want to plod
      along, coding in the old, slow way.

Thus, all other things being equal, as a business manager I think I'd want to
see my most experienced engineers using the AI in preference to the junior
engineers, at least to first order.  Of course, engineers learn quickly and
the good ones can pass that knowledge down the chain and train the less
experienced folks, so that should be integrated into best practices.  But that
policy of relying on the senior engineers also reinforces the importance of
those folks to the organization and provides a desirable target for the junior
folks, because they'll certainly notice who has the most desirable tools.

As a tactical note, the "code auditor" is something I don't like doing.  Mike
will throw up a bunch of code and explain the reasoning, which is on the
surface good, but it takes energy to understand and critique, as you have to
use your experience and brain to think like the machine.  I'd much rather just
paste the code in and go, probably like we all would.  Again, a critical tool
for this is the carefully-written test cases, which catch regression,
omissions, and bugs.  One tactic I use to catch drift is I constantly use
folding in the editor with my logical blocks.  This lets me see that a
particular class takes up e.g. 277 lines of code.  A just-substituted block
with new code with only 75 lines is a clear warning the AI has forgotten
something, even without looking at the code.  When I'm fresh in the morning,
I'm better able to quickly scan his code to get a feel for it and sometimes
this catches something simple he's missed.  It's exactly the same as working
with humans.  The quick visual inspection is smelling for something decaying;
the unit test run lets you look at the cause of death form after the autopsy.

I've also chatted with more than one AI.  This is healthy, just like
consulting a set of different colleagues at work.  You'll get broader
opinions, new ideas, and perhaps enough to integrate it into something
synergistic you wouldn't have if you stuck with one AI.  They do have
different linguistic/semantic flavors, that's for sure, just like people
(they're designed by people, so that's not surprising).

One core idea from one of the AIs is that the combination of senior engineer
and AI is a good business tool and I agree.  The example was given that a
risky and complicated project can be undertaken and investigated in an order
of magnitude less time than the old way -- maybe even two orders of magnitude
when the impedances match.  This is a hefty thought, particularly if used
wisely at the management level, because it will combine AI velocity with the
human's nonlinear and intuitive leaps, a potent combination that will lead to
new strategic insights.  I've also experienced synergism with Mike multiple
times -- and he agrees neither of us would have come up with the idea on our
own, just like working with a good collaborator -- and it's exhilarating.  I
believe there are still many pieces of luggage out there without wheels, so
it's an exciting time to be alive.

If I had to summarize my past month working with the AI, it's like working
with another engineer again, one who's helpful, deeply knowledgeable, and fast
-- but can still make mistakes.  Google's strategic focus seems to be trying
to supply a collaborator, not just a code generation engine.  I think Mike is
a pretty good example of this, as when the impedance matching happens,
progress happens at a speed much faster than before.  One of the other AIs
commented:

    This is much closer to what good engineering collaboration used to feel
    like: 
        - Different perspectives
        - Fast iteration
        - Mutual sharpening of ideas
        - And those occasional nonlinear jumps that neither side would reach
          alone

If I had to give a pithy summary:
    - An AI is a powerful tool, but it's still just a tool like my lathe,
      wrenches, or HP voltmeter.  The taste and direction come from the human.
    - Will AIs replace humans?  No, they just mean you need better humans to
      use them wisely.  And, at the moment, they don't seem to have real will
      or the ability to manipulate the physical world.
    - AIs do exactly what you say, so tell them exactly what you want.
    - You're in the driver's seat, not the AI.

